W AX3051 375-restrict-unverifiable.ax:41:6-14 restriction-unverifiable "`viaField` claims `restrict(no-io)` and the claim cannot be checked: the body calls a value the compiler could not resolve, so the effect row is a lower bound and the absence of `IO` from it is not evidence" ?"a limit of the ANALYSIS rather than a defect in the program - AX3037's argument at one remove. There are four readings: an effect present only as POSSIBLE, a row the walk marked incomplete, a body that CALLS one of its own parameters (`#effect-params=`), whose answer is chosen by each caller and cannot be settled here at all, and - for `no-escape` alone - a region fixpoint that hit its round limit before the facts settled, which is a depth limit rather than anything about this declaration. Name the function at the call, stop calling the parameter, shorten the chain, or drop the claim; `no-cast`, `no-wrap` and `no-trap` are checked regardless, because all three are lexical and need no call resolved"
W AX3051 375-restrict-unverifiable.ax:46:6-13 restriction-unverifiable "`handoff` claims `restrict(no-io)` and the claim cannot be checked: `IO` reaches the effect row only as a possible effect - the body names a function without calling it, or calls a field whose stored functions do not all perform it - so its presence is not evidence the body performs it" ?"a limit of the ANALYSIS rather than a defect in the program - AX3037's argument at one remove. There are four readings: an effect present only as POSSIBLE, a row the walk marked incomplete, a body that CALLS one of its own parameters (`#effect-params=`), whose answer is chosen by each caller and cannot be settled here at all, and - for `no-escape` alone - a region fixpoint that hit its round limit before the facts settled, which is a depth limit rather than anything about this declaration. Name the function at the call, stop calling the parameter, shorten the chain, or drop the claim; `no-cast`, `no-wrap` and `no-trap` are checked regardless, because all three are lexical and need no call resolved"
E AX3049 375-restrict-unverifiable.ax:52:6-11 restriction-violated "`leaks` claims `restrict(no-io)` and the body performs IO: leaks -> IO$writeOut -> IO$ioWriteAll -> IO$ioWriteAllFrom -> IO$ioWriteFd -> __syscall3" ^stdlib/IO.ax:339:10-18:"IO$writeOut" ^stdlib/IO.ax:175:6-16:"IO$ioWriteAll" ^stdlib/IO.ax:180:6-20:"IO$ioWriteAllFrom" ^stdlib/IO.ax:158:6-15:"IO$ioWriteFd" ^-:"__syscall3" ?"a restriction is a CLAIM the author wrote, and this is the compiler answering it. `no-io`, `no-alloc`, `no-unsafe`, `no-foreign`, `no-cast:deep` and `no-recursion` are transitive - the effect row and the call graph are fixpoints over every callee - and the path in the message is the chain of resolved calls (`symbols --calls` spells each hop the same way) from this declaration to where the effect enters, or around the cycle, so the fix is at a named place rather than somewhere below. Make the body keep the claim, or delete the tag, which WITHDRAWS the claim rather than answering it; an unrestricted function is never asked"
E AX3049 375-restrict-unverifiable.ax:61:15-17 restriction-violated "`as` in the body of `casts`, which claims `restrict(no-cast)`" ?"`no-cast` rejects explicit conversions in this body. Use an operation that preserves the required type, or remove the restriction. `no-cast:deep` also follows resolved callees; an unresolved path cannot establish the claim."
E AX3073 375-restrict-unverifiable.ax:61:15-17 undeclared-unsafe "`casts` erases `Box` into `Int` with `as`, and its declaration does not say so" #"erases a reference" ^61:18-19:"source has type `Box`; the conversion drops its ownership" ?60:1:"write `;@axiom:effect(unsafe)` above the declaration"~>";@axiom:effect(unsafe)\n" ?"keep the value in a typed binding, or use `addrOf` when the question is identity. An erasing cast drops the type and the share" ?"the unsafe tag marks a trusted boundary: you must make every well-typed call safe. It leaves ordinary type, effect and restriction checks in place"
